---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../../site.config';

const updated = '6 September 2026';
---

<AppShell
  title={`Extension privacy policy — ${siteConfig.siteName}`}
  description={`Plain-English privacy policy for the ${siteConfig.siteName} Chrome extension and website.`}
>
  <div class="legal-wrap glass card">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <p>
      This policy covers the <strong>{siteConfig.siteName} Chrome extension</strong> (the side panel that scores a
      property listing), and summarises the website account it can hand a deal to. The website's full policy is at
      <a href="/privacy">/privacy</a> and is the authority for everything the website does; this page never contradicts
      it. Both are written in plain English and describe what the software actually does.
    </p>

    <h2>The Chrome extension</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your data stays on your device.</strong> Everything the extension remembers — your default strategy,
        your assumptions and criteria, rents you’ve entered for an area, and per-listing figures — is stored only in
        your browser’s local storage on your own computer. It’s never uploaded to us, and it isn’t synced across your
        devices. Removing the extension removes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It never sends the page’s content anywhere on its own.</strong> The extension reads only the one listing
        page you personally open, in your browser, to pull out figures like the price and size. The listing’s text, exact
        address and price aren’t sent to us or anyone else automatically — the only time those details leave your device
        is when <em>you</em> click “Send to my analyser” (see below). The extension does not scrape: it never crawls the
        portal or opens pages in the background, and it only reads a page when you ask it to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What goes over the network.</strong> To score a deal, the panel looks up recent sold prices for the
        property’s <em>postcode sector</em> (about 2,000 addresses) from our public data store (hosted on Cloudflare). That request includes the
        postcode sector of the property you’re viewing and, like any web request, your device’s IP address — but
        <em>not</em> the listing’s text, exact address, or price. When the panel opens it also refreshes its own
        reading settings from that same Cloudflare data store (a fixed file — your IP only, nothing about the page).
        If you open the measure tool, the panel loads the listing’s floor-plan image from the portal into your browser
        so you can measure it — the image is displayed on your device and nothing is uploaded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Daily reminders, if you leave them on.</strong> Once a day while Chrome is running — and when Chrome
        starts — the panel asks <em>your own signed-in account</em> on our website which of your saved deals need you.
        That is the one request that identifies you: it carries your website sign-in cookie. The reply is a count and,
        if a deal has a date due, that deal’s own line — which can appear once a day as a desktop notification carrying
        the property’s title. The count and which deals you have already been told about are kept on your device.
        Switch it off in the extension’s options and none of it runs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No tracking. No account needed to score a listing.</strong> The extension contains no analytics,
        advertising or tracking of any kind, and can only run its own code. It requests four Chrome permissions — the
        side panel, local storage, a daily alarm and notifications (the last two only for the reminders above) — plus
        access to Rightmove and Zoopla pages and to our own website.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sending a deal to the website.</strong> If you click “Send to my analyser”, the extension opens a new
        browser tab on our website with the deal’s details — the property’s address and price and the figures you entered
        — carried in the web address. This is the one time those details leave your device, and only when you click it. If
        you are then signed in on the website and choose to save the deal, those details are stored in your account
        (see “The website account” below).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The website account</h2>
    <p>You can use the website’s analyser without signing in. If you choose to sign in with Google to save deals, we store:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your <strong>email address</strong> and <strong>name</strong> from your Google account, and your Google profile picture link.</li>
      <li>The <strong>date you signed up</strong>, and — if you ticked the marketing box — a record that you consented, when, and to which version of the wording.</li>
      <li>The <strong>deals you save</strong>: the figures you entered, a label you give the deal, and which strategy it’s for. (A deal you save may include a postcode or address you typed in.)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We do <strong>not</strong> store a password (sign-in is handled by Google) or any payment details. One page asks for a
      phone number — the bridging finance enquiry, because the outcome of that enquiry is a phone call. Nowhere else does.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Where it’s stored.</strong> Your account and saved deals are held in a Cloudflare database created in
      Cloudflare’s <strong>EU jurisdiction</strong>. The UK recognises the EU under “data adequacy”, so this is lawful for
      UK users. There is no UK-only residency option — EU jurisdiction with UK adequacy is the honest position.
    </p>

    <h2>Marketing email</h2>
    <p>
      Every email is sent by our provider, Kit — this app sends none. Kit receives your <strong>email address and first
      name</strong> in three cases: you tick the marketing box, you send a bridging enquiry, or you ask one of the free
      tools to email you its breakdown (which also sends the figures from that answer). Otherwise nothing about you
      reaches Kit. You can turn marketing off any time in your account, which tells Kit to unsubscribe you.
    </p>

    <h2>Signup security</h2>
    <p>
      The first time you create an account, we run a Cloudflare Turnstile anti-bot check. That sends a challenge token
      and your IP address to Cloudflare to confirm you’re a person. Turnstile is not analytics, and returning sign-ins
      are not challenged.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      We set two cookies, both strictly necessary and both hidden from page scripts: one keeps you signed in for 30 days,
      and one is a short-lived security cookie used only during sign-in. Cloudflare’s Turnstile human-check sets its own
      when it runs. There are no advertising or tracking cookies, so there’s no cookie banner to click through.
    </p>

    <h2>Who else sees anything</h2>
    <p>To make the tools work, your browser or our server talks to these services — none of them are advertising or analytics companies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosts the website and our sold-price data, and runs the anti-bot check.</li>
      <li><strong>Google</strong> — only when you sign in; it tells us your email and name.</li>
      <li><strong>Kit</strong> — only if you opt into marketing (email + first name, as above).</li>
      <li><strong>HM Land Registry, police.uk, the Environment Agency, and planning.data.gov.uk</strong> — open-government data. When you analyse a property, your browser asks these for sold prices, crime, flood and planning info for that <em>location</em>. They see the property’s postcode or coordinates and your IP — never your account email or name.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we never do</h2>
    <p>No advertising. No third-party analytics or tracking. We never sell or rent your data to anyone.</p>

    <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
    <p>
      In your account page you can delete your account. That permanently removes your account record, every deal you
      saved, everything in your pipeline, any bridging enquiry you sent and anything a tool queued for your email address,
      and signs you out. If you were opted into marketing, it also queues an unsubscribe to Kit. This can’t be undone.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about your data or this policy? Reach us on
      <a href={siteConfig.socials.instagram} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Instagram</a> or
      <a href={siteConfig.socials.youtube} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</AppShell>

<style>
  .legal-updated { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: var(--text-sm); margin-top: 0; }
</style>
